+3.1 On the original contractible domain, [F3] implies $K_h=V_h\cap B$. If $v_h=\nabla p\in K_h$ with $p\in H_0^1$, then on every tetrahedron $v_h=a+b\times x$ has curl $2b=0$, so it is constant and $p$ is affine there. The piecewise affine representatives have matching traces on each shared face because they represent one $H^1$ function; equality almost everywhere of two affine face polynomials is equality everywhere on that face, including its vertices. At each mesh vertex the incident tetrahedra are connected through shared faces containing that vertex: the link is a triangulated sphere at an interior vertex and a triangulated disk at a boundary vertex, since the matching mesh realizes the Lipschitz three-manifold with boundary. A path in this link, perturbed to miss its finitely many vertices, gives the required face chain. Equality of the affine values therefore propagates to every tetrahedron incident at the vertex. The common nodal values assemble a continuous piecewise affine function on $\overline\Omega$, equal to $p$ almost everywhere, hence in $W_h^0$. Its trace on every open boundary triangle is zero since $p\in H_0^1$; the affine trace polynomial is consequently zero everywhere on that triangle. Every boundary vertex belongs to such a triangle, so all boundary nodal values vanish and the assembled function belongs to $S_h$. Conversely $\nabla S_h\subset K_h$. Consequently $K_h=B_h$, $Z_h=X_h$, and step 2.2 is the asserted inequality on $X_h$. [F3, step 2.2, algebra]
 
